ases so um i think  at the moment  using just these confirmed cases which  is nice for media headlines and  as like deaths doesn't give the full  picture and often exaggerates  rapid changes uh going up or down and so  i think our data on the ons is a better  guide to actually what's happening  and doesn't show as bleaker picture as  often is portrayed with the deaths and  the confirmed cases  um so you know the ideas of these  massive surges in london  um aren't borne out by our data we are  seeing  increases which are worrying but we're  not seeing increases in the over 60s  we're recording hospital admissions  because we were told by the government  that  these five parameters to assess tears  would be transparent and given out to  everybody but that hasn't materialized  so we're  on the app providing that to users and  anyone who logs into our app  gets the full report so we're trying to  be more transparent so people we believe  people will act  responsibly if they're given the data  and at the moment  they're not being given the data they're  just being given a bit of a stick  and so that's uh generally what  we're we're we're up against and i and  looking at our data  over the last few months we were able to  look at certain patterns  and realize that you know certain  decisions about lockdowns etc  um or welsh fire breaks you know  uh have actually failed or were taken  prematurely as katie mentioned earlier  that  sometimes these big decisions are taken  too early without the latest data  and by the time they find out  everything's changed and then  you know the data are retrofitted to to  go with that decision  so um i think you need to look at all  the data  and get also get the most up-to-date  data i think is really important because  there are  huge differences across the country and  we talk about it as if there's only one  you know these massive regions or we're  all  the uk is one thing but there are we're  predicting  by christmas there'll be tenfo