n in this country  where we announce  some new tier system coming in uh and  all the pubs will shut in three days  time  and get surprised that people go out and  meet their mates  um in the next few days and then we lock  them down for again  and we see these very much behavioral  effects that i don't think have been  taken into account so  um looking at the data we'd say it's a  big mistake to keep  turning these screws up and down and  looking at the the first bit of the  second wave it looks like the  uh decision to have a national lockdown  wasn't really justified anyway  because it was coming down in the north  already before that  at quite a fast rate so i think you know  it's easy  with retrospect to look at this and i  think it's a really tricky problem for  government  but i think we do have the data to  enable us to learn lessons going forward  and i think  it would be not to keep turning the tap  on and off it would be to actually  have some regulations until easter that  makes sense  and give people this local information  about what's going on in their hospital  what the rates are  you know what their real risk is and i  think that's  where we're hoping to do that with our  with the zoe app  you would see what you're saying  professor is the the government  needs better data more reliable data on  which to base its decisions  and once it's based decisions on that  more the kind of more reliable the data  you're talking about  there should then should be an element  of consistency  uh in in whatever lockdown or  restrictions  there's going to be that that we should  abandon this  what we've called here on spectator tv  the sort of hokey cookie of in and out  shaken all about would that be a fair  summary of what you're saying  yeah absolutely and i think we have to  also set you know we have to set some  idea what the actual  achieve what's the aim what is the  outcome here  is it just to stop levels you know as  the media calls it surging and everyone 